 el 13 % de los contratos junto al límite, mientras que diciembre supone el 8 % de todos sus contratos de servicios y suministros. Los contratos junto al límite se reparten entre 74 adjudicatarios distintos. El contrato [1], de 14.210 euros, se adjudicó en diciembre de 2025.\nQué no pueden demostrar: Aproximarse al límite no implica irregularidades. Puede deberse a necesidades específicas, presupuestos cerrados o precios del mercado. No confunde cantidad con calidad o procedimiento.\nQué se puede revisar: Comprobar si los contratos de diciembre se reparten entre distintos proveedores. Verificar si el adjudicatario A repite contratos en áreas similares. Analizar si los contratos se agrupan por necesidades comunes.","en":"What the data shows: Between 2023 and 2025, this body awarded 124 minor contracts for services and supplies above 14,000 and up to 15,000 euros, just under the legal limit. If those amounts were spread as in the band immediately below, 24.6 contracts would be expected. The observed-to-expected ratio is 5.0, with a 95% confidence interval of 3.9 to 6.5. December accounts for 13% of the contracts just under the limit, compared with 8% of all its services and supplies contracts. The awardee with the most contracts just under the limit, called awardee A here, has 8. Contract [1], for 14,210 euros, was awarded in December 2025.\nWhat it cannot show: The concentration of contracts in December does not indicate improper activity. Similar patterns can occur naturally due to seasonal demand or budget cycles. The number of contracts does not prove wrongdoing, as market prices or budget constraints may explain the amounts near the limit.\nWhat can be checked: Check the dates and amounts of contracts awarded in December to see if they align with the body’s budget or seasonal needs. Review awardee A’s contracts to determine if they repeat the same service or if they cover multiple distinct requirements. Contract [1] could be a good starting point for this review.